Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCNs) for Forex Forecasting
Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCNs) are a class of 1D-convolutional architectures designed for
sequence modeling and time-series forecasting. Unlike recurrent nets, a TCN uses causal, dilated
convolutions so that each output at time t depends only on inputs ≤ t. By stacking layers with
exponentially increasing dilation, a TCN can cover a large receptive field (long history) with relatively few
layers. For example, Bai et al. (2018) show that a simple TCN “convincingly outperforms” LSTM/GRU models
across many tasks and has longer effective memory. TCNs avoid the vanishing/exploding gradients and
sequential bottleneck of RNNs, allowing parallel computation and stable gradients. In practice, a TCN model
consists of stacked residual blocks of 1D convolutions (often with skip connections) that maintain the input/
output length by padding, and use dilation to expand context.
TCNs are well-suited to short-term FX forecasting for several reasons. First, their convolutional filters can
automatically learn local patterns in the high-frequency price data (filtering out noise) and stack these to
detect longer patterns . Second, the causal/dilated structure explicitly controls memory: by adjusting
dilation and depth, the model can capture dependencies up to minutes or hours in the past. Third, unlike
pure autoregressive models, TCNs can ingest multivariate inputs (e.g. OHLC prices, indicators) with their
multi-channel convolutions. These traits make TCNs competitive for intra-day series (1–5 minute bars)
where both very recent moves and slightly longer context may matter.
Data Sources: High-Resolution Forex Candles
Accurate historical data is essential. Several free or low-cost sources provide minute-level FX quotes:
HistData.com – Offers free tick and 1-minute bar data for many pairs (EUR/USD, USD/JPY, GBP/JPY,
etc.) in CSV files. Data is organized by year/month and includes common majors.
Dukascopy – A Swiss broker with a “Historical Data Feed” providing high-quality data from tick to 1-
min bars. Users can download any pair’s history in .csv or .hst format via their web interface.
Currency API services – Some APIs allow limited free queries: e.g. Alpha Vantage, Twelve Data, or
OANDA’s practice account API can be used to pull 1–5 min FX data (with registration). For example,
Alpha Vantage’s FX endpoint returns intraday bars (subject to rate limits).
Platform data – Many trading platforms (MetaTrader, NinjaTrader, etc.) let you export minute-bars.
For example, MetaTrader’s History Center can export OHLC CSV for any symbol.
Alternate exchanges – Some sites like Stooq or Yahoo Finance offer intraday data (typically 5-min)
for major pairs. Kaggle and GitHub also have community-shared FX datasets (though update
frequency may vary).
When using such sources, check data quality (gaps, spread), and align time zones. For binary-options
simulations, use the bid-side closing prices (or mid) from the broker you plan to trade, since payoffs are
based on where prices settle at expiry.
