Data Preprocessing & Feature Engineering
Before training a TCN, prepare the data as a supervised sequence problem. Common steps include:
Resampling/aligning: If mixing sources (e.g. 1-min OANDA with 3-min MT4), ensure consistent
timestamps and fill any missing candles (forward-fill or drop). Typically use closed candle values
(close price) or OHLCV vectors.
Normalization/Scaling: Neural nets work better with scaled inputs. Convert prices to returns or
normalized values, e.g. log returns or percentage changes, then apply z-score or
min-max scaling (fit on training set only) . This keeps inputs in [-1,1] or similar.
Multivariate channels: Use all relevant price fields as separate channels. A common choice is to
feed the TCN channels = [open, high, low, close, volume] (or fewer). This exploits convolution across
features at each time step. If volume is unavailable (FX often has tick count or “real volume” from
brokers), omit or use tick count as proxy.
Lagged sequences: Format the dataset into overlapping sliding windows. For example, for 1-min
data, a window of length 60 (past 60 minutes) could be used to predict the next candle. Each training
sample is a 3D tensor of shape (channels, sequence_length), and the label is the next period’s
outcome (price or direction).
Technical indicators: Optionally compute features like moving averages, RSI, MACD, ATR, etc., and
append them as additional channels. Research has shown that incorporating statistical/technical
indicators can help capture trends or volatility (e.g. a forex forecasting model used trend/oscillator
indicators to highlight momentum and volatility features). For instance, add a rolling 5-min and 15-
min SMA or recent volatility as extra features. However, note TCNs can often learn similar patterns
from raw prices, so indicators are optional.
Time features: Intraday seasonality can matter (market opens/closes, lunch hours). You may add
features like “minute of day” (normalized cyclically) or binary flags (e.g. London session on/off).
These can be appended as constant features across the window.
In summary, your input tensor for PyTorch would be shape (batch_size, C, L) where C = number of
channels (e.g. 4 for OHLC, or more with indicators) and L = window length. The target can be defined in two
ways: regression (predict next price) or classification (predict up/down direction). For binary options,
classification is common: e.g. label 1 if next close > current close (call wins), else 0.
Model Implementation
Below is an illustrative PyTorch implementation of a simple TCN and training loop. We use nn.Conv1d
with causal/dilated layers to build the network, then add a final linear layer for binary classification. In
practice, one might use a specialized TCN library (e.g. the pytorch-tcn package) or a deeper architecture
with residual blocks.
import torch
import torch.nn as nn
import torch.optim as optim
class SimpleTCN(nn.Module):
def __init__(self, input_channels, num_channels, kernel_size=3,
