Adjust these based on data size and validation performance. Larger models (more layers or channels) can
capture more complexity but risk overfitting on limited data.
Real-Time Prediction and Auto-Trading
To trade in real time, you need a live data feed and a trade execution interface. The loop typically works as:
Fetch live data: At each candle close (every 1 or 3 minutes), retrieve the latest price candle. This can
be done via a broker API (if available) or by pulling from a WebSocket/HTTP data source. For
example, OANDA or Alpaca APIs can stream live FX quotes into Python. If using a platform like
MetaTrader, one could use its Python connector or export data via files. Ensure minimal latency.
Generate prediction: Feed the most recent L-length window of data (reshaped as (1, C, L) ) into
the trained TCN. Get a probability or direction (call/put). E.g.:
