# on each new candle:
x_live = torch.tensor(latest_window, dtype=torch.float).unsqueeze(0) #
shape (1, C, L)
prob = model(x_live).item()
direction = "CALL" if prob > 0.5 else "PUT"
Place trade: Send an order to your trading platform. This is the tricky part for Quotex: as of now,
Quotex does not offer a public API, so automated order placement requires a workaround.
Options include:
Web automation: Use a browser automation tool (Selenium) to log in to Quotex’s web interface and
simulate button clicks at the right time (click “call” or “put” based on direction ).
MetaTrader bridge: Some traders use MT4/MT5 with a bridge plugin (e.g. MT2Trading or
AutoBotSignal) that converts signals into binary trades. These are paid solutions.
Alternative brokers: Consider switching to a broker with an API. For example, Deriv (Binary.com)
has a JSON API that supports binary options trades, and OANDA has an API for vanilla forex (though
not binary) that you could use similarly for directional trading.
Binary Options libraries: There are community libraries (e.g. one on GitHub for IQ Option) that
reverse-engineer the broker’s protocol. Use with caution (can violate terms of service).
Execution timing: For 1–3 minute options, timing is crucial. Ensure your trade command reaches
the broker before the next candle forms. Account for any latency. Many systems wait a few seconds
after candle close to confirm the final price before deciding.
A pseudocode loop:
while True:
candle = get_latest_candle()
window = update_window_with(candle)
pred = model.predict(window)
if pred > 0.5:
place_order("CALL", expiration=1min)
else:
place_order("PUT", expiration=1min)
sleep(remaining_seconds_to_next_candle)
Note: Automated trading with real money carries significant risk. Always paper-trade first. If doing this in
production, handle exceptions (disconnects, bad data) carefully, and follow the broker’s rate limits. Respect
trading session times (markets may close on weekends or holidays).
