Evaluation, Backtesting, and Risks
Robust evaluation is critical. Split your historical data into chronological train/validation/test sets (e.g. train
on 2020-2022, validate on 2023, test on 2024). Use walk-forward (rolling) validation to mimic live
conditions: repeatedly train on data up to time t, test on t+1…t+k. Ensure no lookahead bias (don’t shuffle
time series; respect order) .
Backtest by simulating trades: apply the model’s signal to past data and calculate what the payoff would
have been (accounting for binary payout ratios and fees). Common metrics include accuracy, precision, and
especially profitability metrics (win rate, profit factor, Sharpe ratio on returns). For binary options, even a
50% accuracy may be losing if payouts <1:1, so examine net ROI.
Always reserve a strictly out-of-sample test period that the model never saw until final testing. This
assesses real-world performance. Consider using cross-validation tailored to time series (not random folds)
to tune hyperparameters.
Risks and precautions: Binary options are high-risk, short-duration bets. Regulators emphasize their
dangers (in fact, many jurisdictions ban them). Key cautions:
Overfitting: Deep models can memorize noise in 1-min data. Use dropout and early stopping, and
check that model performance is consistent over different years.
Slippage and execution delays: The model’s prediction assumes execution at the closing price. In
reality, there may be latency or spread. This can erode small edges.
Trade size and money management: Never bet more than a small fraction of capital on any trade.
Use fixed fractional sizing or the Kelly criterion conservatively. Binary options have “all-or-nothing”
payoffs, so a string of losses can wipe out gains. Incorporate stop-loss or daily loss limits at the
portfolio level.
Changing market conditions: Models trained on historical patterns may fail in regime shifts (e.g.
news events, volatility spikes). Retrain periodically with new data.
Confirmation and ensemble: It can help to require the model’s confidence to exceed a threshold
before trading, or to combine signals from multiple timeframes or models.
Legality and broker rules: Ensure your broker allows automated trading. Some forbid bots and may
flag accounts for suspicious trading patterns.
In summary, while a TCN can learn complex short-term dependencies in Forex data, its predictions are
not guarantees. Thorough backtesting, realistic simulations (including commission/spread), and prudent
risk management are essential. Always interpret results critically and be prepared for losses.
Sources: TCN architecture and advantages; free FX data (HistData, Dukascopy); feature examples ;
binary options risk warnings. Each section above reflects these published insights and common industry
